{\b Chapter 6 Discussion and Conclusion}\par
\par
{\b 6.1 Introduction}\par
This chapter discusses the main findings of the study in relation to the theoretical framework, prior literature, and the broader context of IT micro-credentials in Thailand. It then outlines the theoretical, practical, and policy implications of the study, identifies limitations, offers recommendations for future research, and concludes the thesis.\par
\par
{\b 6.2 Discussion of Findings}\par
This section should interpret the findings reported in Chapter 5 rather than repeat the statistical outputs. Each major result should be discussed in relation to the theories and studies reviewed in Chapters 2 and 3.\par
\par
Suggested subsections:\par
{\b 6.2.1 Recognition and Student Trust}\par
Discuss whether recognition increased trust and how this aligns with signaling theory, legitimacy logic, and prior literature on recognition and institutional meaning.\par
\par
{\b 6.2.2 Stackability and Student Trust}\par
Discuss whether stackability functioned as a meaningful signal of future opportunity and how this connects to pathway logic in the micro-credential literature.\par
\par
{\b 6.2.3 Industry Endorsement and Student Trust}\par
Discuss whether endorsement acted as a credibility cue and how this relates to employability and external validation.\par
\par
{\b 6.2.4 Student Trust and Perceived Value}\par
Explain whether trust emerged as the central mechanism connecting visible credential features to value judgments.\par
\par
{\b 6.2.5 Direct Effects on Perceived Value}\par
Interpret the direct effects of recognition, stackability, and industry endorsement on perceived value, especially where direct and indirect effects differ.\par
\par
{\b 6.2.6 Mediation of Student Trust}\par
Discuss whether trust functioned as a full or partial mediator and what this means for understanding how students evaluate micro-credentials.\par
\par
{\b 6.3 Theoretical Implications}\par
This section should explain what the study contributes to theory. The discussion should emphasize how the results extend current understanding of micro-credentials beyond simple adoption models and toward a student-centered model of signal interpretation, legitimacy, trust, and perceived value.\par
\par
Suggested points:\par
- implications for signaling theory in educational credentials\par
- implications for legitimacy and trust in emerging credential systems\par
- implications for perceived value as a stronger student-side outcome\par
\par
{\b 6.4 Practical Implications}\par
This section should explain what the findings mean for universities, online learning providers, and designers of IT micro-credentials. The main focus should be on how to design credentials that students are more likely to trust and value.\par
\par
Suggested points:\par
- importance of formal recognition\par
- value of clear pathway and stackability design\par
- role of external industry endorsement and communication\par
- implications for micro-credential marketing and presentation to students\par
\par
{\b 6.5 Policy Implications}\par
This section should connect the findings to lifelong learning, flexible pathways, and skills policy in Thailand. The emphasis should be on what policymakers can learn about recognition systems, credit-bank structures, and communication of micro-credential value.\par
\par
Suggested points:\par
- implications for the National Credit Bank System\par
- implications for university recognition policies\par
- implications for national digital skills and lifelong learning strategies\par
\par
{\b 6.6 Limitations}\par
This section should acknowledge the main limitations of the study without undermining the study's value.\par
\par
Suggested points:\par
- student-only respondents\par
- stated perceptions rather than actual labor-market outcomes\par
- hypothetical vignette judgments rather than real enrollment behavior\par
- non-probability sampling\par
- Thailand-specific context\par
\par
{\b 6.7 Recommendations for Future Research}\par
This section should propose realistic next steps for future research.\par
\par
Suggested directions:\par
- replicate the model in other national contexts\par
- include employer or university decision-maker samples\par
- examine real enrollment or hiring outcomes\par
- test additional signals such as issuer type, transcript status, or assessment rigor\par
- compare different disciplinary groups beyond IT students\par
\par
{\b 6.8 Conclusion}\par
This section should provide the final closing argument of the thesis. It should restate the problem, summarize the main contribution, and explain why the study matters. The conclusion should end with the central message that the value of IT micro-credentials is not judged only by content or convenience, but also by the visible signals that shape student trust and perceived value.\par
}
